From the P?S8 Google group

---------- Forwarded message ---------
From: cjl <Unknown>
Date: Wednesday, February 7, 2018 at 9:17:22 AM UTC-6
Subject: P?S/8 manuals being updated.
To: P?S/8 <Unknown>


Certain individuals asked for this, just before this awful cold weather in 
the northeast, etc. which is greatly affecting me.

This is the current status of writing:

The P?S/8 PAL Technical reference is essentially complete; it is in final 
pre-release awaiting some typo etc. checking from some others.  This is 
important if you use PAL8 as well; a lot of basic assembler how-to and what 
not to do is generic to several PDP-8 assemblers and in some cases there 
are direct feature comparisons between P?S/8 PAL, OS/8 PAL8 and TOPS-10 
PAL10.  There isn't anything else quite like it I've ever come across 
[which is why it is not merely a feature by feature manual.  Of course it 
has all the descriptions of all the directives, including some future ones, 
and a complete listing of all the error messages.

I will be making a more formal announcement after they finish "beating it 
up" for mistakes, etc.

P?S/8 lacks a program to process ASCII text paper-tape into P?S/8 files and 
vice-versa.  The work-around is to use OS/8 and its high -speed paper-tape 
reader/punch handlers and use the existing OS8CON to interchange files 
between the two systems.  Once this is done, there is no user-level 
dependence on OS/8 for any normal reason, etc.

This is especially critical in the SIMH world because passing files from 
the Windows host environment depends on the simulated paper-tape reading 
and writing, thus using P?S/8 with host editing is similarly affected, etc.

I have been testing key components of the PTUTIL program that solves this 
entirely and includes many options.  Although the code is not finished, I 
quickly realized this is one of those programs where a lot of time will be 
wasted unless the proper spec for the program is complete.  [Many 
[programmers can relate to "floating" project requirements causing them to 
never get done.]

As such, even though a goodly percentage of the code is done, I am not 
working on it at all. Instead, I have [hopefully] finished the PTUTIL 
manual.  Once it is in the same shape as the PAL manual, I will release it 
also, which will help to explain the problem and how it completely solves 
all issues [and in a much more comprehensive way than OS/8 does].

Note: P?S/8 includes sufficient support for BINARY paper-tapes currently.  
Any conceivable operation regarding binary paper-tapes in either BIN or RIM 
format is supported by the P?S/8 binary file loading program *BIN* with 
various additional option switches set.

You can do such as:

1) Punch the binary output of an assembly to either BIN or RIM on the 
low-speed or high-speed punch.  [In fact you can CHAIN to BIN and do it 
from PAL all in one step.]

2) BIN or RIM files can be loaded and written to P?S/8 output files using 
either the low or high-speed reader.

3) A special version of BIN and RIM and a bootstrap back to P?S/8 can be 
loaded into 07600-07777.  Using option switches, both BIN and RIM are set 
to either the high-speed version or the low-speed version.  [The only 
missing "feature" is the ability to use the front panel from BIN to change 
devices in memory, something that no one ever uses.  The original BIN is 
designed to be stand-alone, but this is ALWAYS loaded from P?S/8 so there 
is no point.  Moreover, there are machines that do not have front panels, 
so this eliminated the entire problem.]  The bootstrap is P?S/8 
configuration-specific, but starting at 07600 will bring back up what you 
were running without writing on the device.

Of special note:  This is not "regular" RIM format but enhanced RIM 
format.  The difference is it also supports a checksum so it can ALSO be 
loaded by the BIN loader.  [Note: Some people create such files in source 
form by using *. instructions before every word of the code.]


BIN has many other options as well not related to paper-tape, but to memory 
loading [its main purpose].

I hope to get to another manual that explains all options of everything as 
a stop-gap manual for everything else.

Consider this a heads-up, but it's out of my hands for the moment.  Once we 
have hashed out any last-second things, it will be released and I will make 
an announcement here.

My current project is documenting the PEPS package which includes a release 
of P/S/8 within it.  It's coming along fine, but this is a rather elaborate 
manual due to circumstances, some originally unintended.  Unfortunately, 
there is the specter of greedy lawyers in the picture because I use several 
third-party Windows applications.  As such, I cannot distribute their 
products, [but I can document how an end user can integrate them into PEPS; 
if they want to go after all of my users, they cannot expect me to police 
their mediocre shareware/freeware.  When I started programming, I would 
never have thought you need to CYA, but welcome to 2018!

cjl

